    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Both parts of the change work, and `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests in the spec.\n\n**Edit conflict (stored data, server and screen)**\n- **Stored data:** a new migration, `V11__person_version.sql`, gives each person a `version` number that starts at 0.\n- **Server:** people are now sent to the screen with their version, and the edit form sends back the version it loaded. A new step, `CheckPersonVersion`, refuses the save with a 409 if the version is out of date. The save also only writes when the version still matches, so two saves landing at almost the same moment are caught too. Either way the refusal happens before the audit step, so nothing is stored or written to the audit file.\n- **Screen:** when a save is refused this way, the form shows the server's message in `person-form-error`, re-reads the sheet, and fills in the current name, grade and version. Pressing Edit again now reloads the editor even for the person already open, so the next save goes through.\n\n**Double submit**\n- A new helper, `api/useSingleSubmit.ts`, ignores a second submit while the first is still waiting for an answer. Disabling the button wasn't enough on its own, because a double click can land before the button re-renders as disabled.\n- I applied it to the four forms that store something: person, claim, rate and sheet-today. The sign-in and sheet-picker forms store nothing, so I left them alone.\n\nThe double-submit guard is only in the browser: the server would still accept a genuine duplicate request from somewhere else. Stopping that would mean adding a request token on the server side, which this change didn't need.",
